{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Therefore you shall place these words of Mine</b> — even after you are exiled, be punctilious in the commandments: lay tefillin, make a mezuzah, so that these not be unfamiliar to you when you return. And so it says (ibid. v. 20): \"Set up waymarks for yourself\" — the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 11:18). I have already written the explanation of the matter, for these commandments are personal obligations (חובת הגוף) and their law applies in every place just as in the Land; but there is in this midrash a deep secret (סוד עמוק), and I have already alluded to it from it (Leviticus 18:25).\n\nNow, He brought back these commandments — tefillin, words of Torah, and mezuzah — here a second time, to hint through this juxtaposition that we are obligated in them after the exile outside the Land; and from them we learn regarding all the commandments that are personal obligations that their obligation applies in every place, and that we are exempt outside the Land from obligations dependent on the land (חובת הקרקע), such as terumah and tithes — so it is expounded in the Sifrei (Ekev 17).\nThe intent of the midrash is because He juxtaposed \"and you shall place\" to \"and you shall perish,\" but the primary reference of the verse is in the Land, as it is written (Deuteronomy 11:21): \"that your days may be multiplied and the days of your children upon the soil (האדמה).\" Or one may say <b>that your days may be multiplied</b> — that you shall return and prolong your days upon it forever.\n\nIt is also possible by way of the plain sense (דרך הפשט) that He came to add here to speak of them, for there (Deuteronomy 6:7) He commanded \"and you shall speak of them when you sit in your house,\" whereas here He said that we must teach them to our children until the children speak of them at all times. And so He added here \"and you shall teach them,\" for <b>and you shall teach them (ושננתם)</b> means that one tells them the commandments, whereas here it is until they learn them and know them and understand them and their reasons, to speak of them with you at all times. And so He added here \"as the days of the heavens upon the earth,\" which means for generation after generation — or these are the first heavens and earth and the days of eternity, and the enlightened will understand (והמשכיל יבין):"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The citation '(שם שם כ)' refers to the same chapter and verse 20; resolved to Deuteronomy 11:20. The citation '(ויקרא יח כה)' is rendered as Leviticus 18:25. The abbreviation ספרי (Sifrei) is kept as the name of the midrash collection."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:15.514477349Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}